{
	title: 'Web completion list you can tap',
	spec: "On the web, typing a slash command or path shows its completions as a list above the prompt, like Slack's (each row: name, and a quieter description line), without pressing Tab (phones have none). Tap or click a row to take it; Up/Down and Enter pick on a keyboard; Escape or typing past the choices hides it. It uses the same completion source as the terminal (common/completion). Replaces the notice line that listed choices (questions.md 104, 125). The menu is a stable display, not a loading indicator: a keystroke never clears visible choices merely to wait for the host. A longer prefix filters already-known candidates immediately when possible; a host answer confirms or changes them. If the menu contents and selection are unchanged, keep the same menu and row DOM identity and the composer at the same position, even during a pending request and after its identical reply. Outdated replies never roll the menu back; genuine dismissal (Escape, send, non-completable text) still closes it. Tests hold the host reply between keystrokes and verify the list never transiently disappears, then verify an identical reply creates no DOM mutation and a different answer updates the choices.",
